#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082925 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/id #uuid "b293a58f-0903-5ee7-ab4b-3cbc2e5e4b3d" :question/math-academy-id "e-560" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of  the curve $y=x^{3/2}$ between the points $x=0$ and $x=\\dfrac{4}{3},$ as shown below.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/assets/4ed8b94a4fff3c22697264e467f955f2e51a62618d03295c4e4f38493bff2ced.png)" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)= x^{3/2},$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\dfrac{4}{3}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) =   \\dfrac{3}{2}x^{1/2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt { 1 + [f'(x)]^2 }\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{3}{2}{x}^{1/2}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{9}{4}x} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{4+9x}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}(4+9x)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}}\\sqrt{4+9x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt { 1 + [f'(x)]^2 } = \\dfrac{1}{2}\\sqrt{4 + 9x}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\,dx.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe evaluate this integral using the substitution $u= 4+9x.$ Then, $\\textrm{d}x = \\dfrac{1}{9}\\textrm{d}u.$\n\nWe also need to compute new limits:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & 0 & \\frac{4}{3} \\\\ u & 4 & 16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\frac{4}{3}}\\sqrt{4+9x}\\,dx \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{4}^{16}\\sqrt{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{9}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{\\int }_{4}^{16}{u}^{\\frac{1}{2}}\\,du \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}{[\\frac{2}{3}{u}^{\\frac{3}{2}}]}_{4}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{18}\\cdot \\frac{2}{3}{[{u}^{\\frac{3}{2}}]}_{4}^{16} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}([(16)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]-[(4)^{\\frac{3}{2}}]) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}([64]-[8]) \\\\  & =\\frac{56}{27}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186082926 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/id #uuid "cf9adf09-b14f-5b5b-a110-60354b93c241" :question/math-academy-id "e-561" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\displaystyle  \\int_{5}^{6} } \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve? \n\n- $y=  \\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}$\n\n- $y=  \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5$\n\n- $y=  \\dfrac{x}{x^2-16} + 1$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL=\\int_{5}^{6}  \\sqrt{1+\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}}  \\,\\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[f'(x)]^2=\\dfrac {x^2}{x^2-16}\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad f'(x) = \\pm \\dfrac {x}{\\sqrt{x^2-16}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{x}{\\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{{x}^{2}-16}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=  \\sqrt{x^2-16} + \\sqrt 5.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083015 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/id #uuid "80794b09-e0f4-5bff-8c0c-8261638f9613" :question/math-academy-id "e-7268" :question/problem "What is the arc length of the curve $y = \\arccos{x}$ on $\\left[0,\\dfrac{1}{2}\\right]?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL = \\int _a^b \\sqrt{1 + [f'(x)]^2} \\, \\textrm{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)= \\arccos{x} ,$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\dfrac{1}{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x)\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\nf'(x) =-\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $f'(x) = -\\dfrac{1}{\\sqrt{1-x^2}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{1-{x}^{2}+1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{\\frac{2-{x}^{2}}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386942 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "406028df-0352-560b-9b42-264aa756c1a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386944} {:answer/id #uuid "47a372f6-4233-5266-93f3-ef8753a1eb85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\sqrt{3-2x}+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386945} {:answer/id #uuid "1534a69f-f3d9-5b03-8edb-768a987593bd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\sqrt{1-2{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386946} {:answer/id #uuid "cd1aa5b0-9c65-56ac-bb38-4174d31bdf2f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\sqrt{2-{x}^{3}}+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386947} {:answer/id #uuid "2e775025-e28f-5a75-b066-0119bf43609d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\sqrt{3+{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "406028df-0352-560b-9b42-264aa756c1a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386944} :answer-field/id #uuid "38800ebd-2861-5022-8a8b-fee3be9419b9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386943}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e9f04453-3f40-58db-b58f-2274524d2d22" :question/math-academy-id "q-122391" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{3-2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{-1}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{3-2{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{4{x}^{2}}{3-2{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{2x}{\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=-\\sqrt{3-2{x}^{2}}+\\sqrt{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186325443 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91f5bc72-7a18-54b9-a102-577832861b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325445} {:answer/id #uuid "b2f99572-65b3-5bf9-9c0f-a70538c178a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y={cos}^{2}⁡x+1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325446} {:answer/id #uuid "607710bd-30b2-519e-b72c-a742e6794283" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x-\\pi " :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325447} {:answer/id #uuid "78121f4f-47a1-541f-972f-e9d12f52e9b4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{sin}⁡x+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325448} {:answer/id #uuid "a187b14f-380c-54f2-8562-6442ae532af8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325449}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "91f5bc72-7a18-54b9-a102-577832861b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325445} :answer-field/id #uuid "b6fad4bb-6159-5bc5-8de7-a37512e9b550" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186325444}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "66e6fd67-7c2f-53c8-9b0c-0cf636baf830" :question/math-academy-id "q-122393" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}={sin}^{2}⁡x\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\operatorname{sin}⁡x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{cos}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡x-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186298873 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85986fd5-08a8-5cb5-bc7b-4d7cfa03ce99" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{1+2x}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298875} {:answer/id #uuid "24b8a9a1-806f-50a4-883e-c0058238a262" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+2{x}^{2}}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298876} {:answer/id #uuid "920f66a7-3653-5bbf-841c-9f51605b83f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\frac{\\sqrt{1+{x}^{2}}}{x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298877} {:answer/id #uuid "086f4687-56dd-5157-9925-0fb15417116b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298878} {:answer/id #uuid "08677152-56be-5b14-bc37-5e67820853c4" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{1+{x}^{2}}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298879}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "086f4687-56dd-5157-9925-0fb15417116b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298878} :answer-field/id #uuid "543b7657-a8b4-5a21-a6e3-d3972662674c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186298874}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e5cbeecb-a1f1-54e5-8cbe-522e7705856c" :question/math-academy-id "q-15315" :question/problem "Which of the following gives the length of the curve $y=2\\sqrt{x}$ on $[0,1]?$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=2\\sqrt{x},$ $a=0,$ and $b=1.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2}{2\\sqrt{x}}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{\\frac{1+x}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386949 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f2395032-bbe5-5dc7-baec-910b13602072" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{cos}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386951} {:answer/id #uuid "b21b4783-aa49-5d41-ae9d-2e144b9f8a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386952} {:answer/id #uuid "4aab0848-19ea-5f74-8bca-790848eaecd1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{sin}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386953} {:answer/id #uuid "f000f73e-e82a-5621-94da-35fee0550957" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+4{cos}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386954} {:answer/id #uuid "69369c60-36e9-5995-b2f4-0974a1b7acd3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+4{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386955}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b21b4783-aa49-5d41-ae9d-2e144b9f8a43" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386952} :answer-field/id #uuid "f904054a-52e2-5724-a79c-646dff7d72f5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386950}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "898e2d00-d1c0-5287-b1c1-c8753234b0c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-15405" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/assets/f74f28bf0b5bf2208b3cebf837c667cf9a9ae737d7eb758708a98548ffca8e3a.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=4{sin}^{2}⁡x$ on $[-\\frac{\\pi }{2},\\frac{\\pi }{2}],$ as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=4{sin}^{2}⁡x,$ $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=4\\cdot 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /2}^{\\pi /2}\\sqrt{1+16{sin}^{2}⁡(2x)}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386956 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1916daff-8ec2-549c-89d4-471a53de781f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{2}-e^{-2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386958}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1916daff-8ec2-549c-89d4-471a53de781f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{e^{2}-e^{-2}}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386958} :answer-field/id #uuid "7839e8fd-b1aa-5a55-8b22-9ada1841643a" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386957}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ff72803f-2d3d-5cc6-b5c3-fcf617357553" :question/math-academy-id "q-352924" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{8}$ for $x\\in [0,\\frac{1}{2}].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{8},$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\frac{1}{2}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{2})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{{e}^{8x}-2+{e}^{-8x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{e}^{8x}+2+{e}^{-8x}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x})^{2}}{4}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{1/2}\\frac{{e}^{4x}+{e}^{-4x}}{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{e}^{4x}-{e}^{-4x}}{8})|}_{0}^{1/2} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{e}^{4(1/2)}-{e}^{-4(1/2)}}{8}]-[\\frac{{e}^{4(0)}-{e}^{-4(0)}}{8}] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{8}({e}^{2}-\\frac{1}{{e}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{4}-1}{8{e}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386959 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a267970-fb52-5c96-bbf8-30c567eef7db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386961}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1a267970-fb52-5c96-bbf8-30c567eef7db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\ln(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386961} :answer-field/id #uuid "2f625505-43f9-5735-96aa-6a3eb36b033f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386960}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e41c732f-7225-5d09-880d-7d7b833ee64e" :question/math-academy-id "q-353191" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)$ for $x\\in [1,3].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x),$ $a=1,$ and $b=3.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{x}{4}-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{2}}{16}+\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{{x}^{4}+8{x}^{2}+16}{16{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{({x}^{2}+4)^{2}}{16{x}^{2}}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{3}(\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)|}_{1}^{3} \\\\  & =[\\frac{{3}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3]-[\\frac{{1}^{2}}{8}+\\operatorname{ln}⁡1] \\\\  & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡3.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386962 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39ca2ec5-8a34-50d2-a165-7227d126a5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386964}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "39ca2ec5-8a34-50d2-a165-7227d126a5ee" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386964} :answer-field/id #uuid "25b10528-df5c-5479-b078-dad4157f08bb" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386963}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b4767432-922a-5be8-be5b-18e91bf8210c" :question/math-academy-id "q-362543" :question/problem "Compute the arc length $L$ of $y=\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)^{3/2}$ for $x\\in [3,6].$ Do not round the answer.\n\n$L=$ \n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}(x-3)^{3/2},$ $a=3,$ and $b=6.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{2}{3}\\cdot \\frac{3}{2}(x-3)^{1/2}=\\sqrt{x-3}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\sqrt{x-3}]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+(x-3)} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{x-2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\sqrt{x-2}$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{3}^{6}\\sqrt{x-2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\frac{2}{3}(x-2)^{3/2})|}_{3}^{6} \\\\  & =[\\frac{2}{3}(6-2)^{3/2}]-[\\frac{2}{3}(3-2)^{3/2}] \\\\  & =\\frac{16}{3}-\\frac{2}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{14}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386965 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "a953e404-68ed-5802-9fcc-bc892f73a8ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{cot}^{4}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386967} {:answer/id #uuid "f7f67879-67cf-5397-8314-9e41e7f438c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386968} {:answer/id #uuid "30ee5108-4913-52ff-9a03-401ef77cfe5c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{cot}^{2}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386969} {:answer/id #uuid "39e901d5-0d91-5fb0-8e9a-43af7eed520e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sin}^{2}⁡x-{cos}^{2}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386970} {:answer/id #uuid "b99215f4-7394-5ffd-91c6-6a67ef6a4ff3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{{sec}^{2}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386971}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f7f67879-67cf-5397-8314-9e41e7f438c0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386968} :answer-field/id #uuid "7a7b1185-6d92-5063-a513-69478aae2c6a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386966}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "d49288fb-58db-5dbb-bf7b-d975ead2b1e6" :question/math-academy-id "q-51270" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14082094/assets/87dc32f8909ac1c05adc981b4133a3f6e721918e4aca6923b08a073d2fb8cfe9.png)\n\nWhich of the following gives the length of the curve $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x$ on $[-\\frac{\\pi }{4},\\frac{\\pi }{3}],$ as shown above?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\operatorname{tan}⁡x,$ $a=-\\frac{\\pi }{4},$ and $b=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)={sec}^{2}⁡x$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1+{[{f}^{′}(x)]}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{-\\pi /4}^{\\pi /3}\\sqrt{1+{sec}^{4}⁡x}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186384391 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de481db3-c405-5a31-8a80-acd98c2f1a9c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{8}\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384393} {:answer/id #uuid "b00cecac-8428-564d-997a-c8a9dcd92e8f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{14}{29}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384394} {:answer/id #uuid "bb4a88bf-7563-502c-8b76-4b411f1d217e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{4}+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384395} {:answer/id #uuid "07c02ea3-54d2-53c4-97d1-e71d7eebc272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384396} {:answer/id #uuid "640f9827-b5a6-5261-8dde-58b6a8cba530" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384397}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07c02ea3-54d2-53c4-97d1-e71d7eebc272" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\frac{15}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384396} :answer-field/id #uuid "2e388846-ab83-514e-8fda-927ace8262c8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186384392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "430a9509-653c-5419-a6b3-7926dc6bcc6a" :question/math-academy-id "q-5998" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ for $x\\in [1,4].$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2}-\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $a=1,$ and $b=4.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{1}{x}\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{4}x-\\frac{1}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}-\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{16}{x}^{2}+\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}}+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+\\frac{16}{{x}^{2}}+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4}\\sqrt{{(\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{{x}^{2}+4}{4x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{4}\\frac{x}{4}+\\frac{1}{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+\\frac{1}{8}{x}^{2})|}_{1}^{4} \\\\  & =[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+\\frac{1}{8}(4)^{2}]-[\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+\\frac{1}{8}(1)^{2}] \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡1-\\frac{1}{8} \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡4+\\frac{15}{8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386972 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dfe835db-c824-5736-ba79-0a0057ae6d64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{\\frac{3\\sqrt[3]{x}}{x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386974} {:answer/id #uuid "a7005d7d-f080-5ad1-b6f6-4689db032325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{9x\\sqrt[3]{x}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386975} {:answer/id #uuid "abe59f73-92ec-5adc-80e4-69b8717d28a3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+9x\\sqrt[3]{x}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386976} {:answer/id #uuid "50734827-1745-569d-b07c-e927ba0a9ee6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386977} {:answer/id #uuid "698b1bc5-19f7-522d-aef2-d12b0ad1c586" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{\\frac{9}{\\sqrt[3]{x}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386978}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a7005d7d-f080-5ad1-b6f6-4689db032325" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{9x\\sqrt[3]{x}}}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386975} :answer-field/id #uuid "829978b1-d0e2-55f5-8907-fef6802eee0a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "75f3ee9a-aa46-5e51-9545-c1a50545e935" :question/math-academy-id "q-6000" :question/problem "The length of the curve $y=\\sqrt[3]{x}$ on $[1,9]$ is given by" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=1$ and $x=9$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\sqrt[3]{x},$ $a=1,$ and $b=9.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}}$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+{(\\frac{1}{3\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{9}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{9x\\sqrt[3]{x}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386979 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "965026e6-db46-5930-8844-c423e6ea795a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding an Integral Representation For an Arc Length"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "85c58e77-01db-58f4-a1af-93af085a6d82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386981} {:answer/id #uuid "fa08814c-ef55-5679-8dac-0f5e4701bb17" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{2{ln}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386982} {:answer/id #uuid "eb74e63a-336e-5857-9d13-94aafe1b39a7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386983} {:answer/id #uuid "208fbe3b-e9d9-5313-a671-3c5e77ed8076" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{2{ln}^{2}⁡x+\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386984} {:answer/id #uuid "08c07a39-c64a-5c5c-8eef-6b63f67e79f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386985}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "85c58e77-01db-58f4-a1af-93af085a6d82" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "{\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386981} :answer-field/id #uuid "2b36a1ad-cc83-5720-9512-b6cd29c942c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386980}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1018367e-83ad-5af3-99ec-2abf57016cee" :question/math-academy-id "q-6001" :question/problem "The length of the curve $y=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x$ on $[1,e]$ is given by" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=x\\operatorname{ln}⁡x,$ $a=1,$ and $b=e.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+x\\cdot \\frac{1}{x}=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${f}^{′}(x)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1$ into the arc length formula, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{1+{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={\\int }_{1}^{e}\\sqrt{{ln}^{2}⁡x+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+2}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083742 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ac8a4ea9-cd1f-575b-acc6-2518eec4f4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3}{2}e^{2x} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081819} {:answer/id #uuid "be15bad8-ab4f-5eee-9c4d-d33beb010987" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{2}e^{2x} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081820} {:answer/id #uuid "e89e3846-fe1b-5de9-9901-dc5003382e00" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3}{2}e^{2x} + \\frac{e^{x}}{2} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081821} {:answer/id #uuid "fa17ed17-c008-5ba8-b071-1337c8be8de3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{1}{2}e^{4x} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081822} {:answer/id #uuid "12612b39-9b02-5941-bc7e-3ab1a972ed67" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3}{2}e^{4x} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081823}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac8a4ea9-cd1f-575b-acc6-2518eec4f4f0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y = \\frac{3}{2}e^{2x} + 1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081819} :answer-field/id #uuid "33a47d8d-5346-5191-a099-0c2fdb2f5314" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186081818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "74becc4a-8ddf-5d15-9e77-edc438c95718" :question/math-academy-id "q-6002" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{1+9{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{5}\\sqrt{1+9{e}^{4x}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=9{e}^{4x}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 3{e}^{2x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 3\\int {e}^{2x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2x}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2x}+1.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386986 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "91e0efce-7970-5889-b19f-8b881fa5c09e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=2+\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386988} {:answer/id #uuid "3507f5be-3820-52e2-bac3-811e6a93755b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(1-{x}^{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386989} {:answer/id #uuid "eae7ea55-0ff8-5a7a-b787-d86136150866" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386990} {:answer/id #uuid "726a300f-c2d6-5c5c-90e8-1f673dd48c92" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{2}+\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386991} {:answer/id #uuid "36bdb045-5075-5d8e-9b5b-25d6e4979ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{2}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386992}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "36bdb045-5075-5d8e-9b5b-25d6e4979ebb" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{2}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386992} :answer-field/id #uuid "6a1fbe26-7b49-5b03-a95c-68dd80ec0e4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386987}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "2465b53b-d454-5ce5-bc18-251befc3123f" :question/math-academy-id "q-82616" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{-1/2}^{1/2}\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=\\frac{1}{1-{x}^{2}}\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm \\int \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{1-{x}^{2}}}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm \\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=\\sqrt{2}-\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186386993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "55f783c4-25d6-5855-877e-31bed16b12c4" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Possible Curve Given Its Arc Length As an Integral"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "633b907e-f199-5825-9546-8fc2bea6ce64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{x}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386995} {:answer/id #uuid "81811a9f-6050-541e-9579-ad6e6ef455c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386996} {:answer/id #uuid "f1212beb-4cff-5f08-b365-9c5fdc91561d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=3\\sqrt{x}-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386997} {:answer/id #uuid "1c0df7f5-977a-5c32-b067-c2c04160369e" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386998} {:answer/id #uuid "e972a5dd-b734-589d-ae62-e56c0c98d599" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{x}}+2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386999}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "81811a9f-6050-541e-9579-ad6e6ef455c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=-2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386996} :answer-field/id #uuid "09ab57cc-af45-5f62-bd67-c166dd3b3717" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186386994}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "70181cb8-d0db-5bb5-b812-c66d19c2730e" :question/math-academy-id "q-82639" :question/problem "The length of a curve $y=f(x)$ is given by ${\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+9x}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Which of the following could be  an equation for this curve?" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe're given that \n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{0}^{1}\\sqrt{1+9x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nComparing the above with the arc length formula, we see that we must have\n\n$$\n[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}=9x\\,⟹\\,{f}^{′}(x)=\\pm 3\\sqrt{x}.\n$$\n\nIntegrating the above equation with respect to $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =\\pm 3\\int \\sqrt{x}\\text{ d}x \\\\  & =\\pm 2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the given options, the only one that has the required form is $y=-2\\sqrt{{x}^{3}}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083860 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "f87bc14d-5e68-5a5e-bec8-f1b5b21c02e8" :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Arc Length of a Smooth Curve Using the Arc Length Formula"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "98a219b4-f3ad-55c3-b4cd-03a3b336ea0c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082527} {:answer/id #uuid "e8960097-daf1-5dbf-8420-24b2759512c5" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082528} {:answer/id #uuid "eb9e6a45-67c1-57c9-8453-f2d9c3411d97" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082529} {:answer/id #uuid "2b83c98e-2aad-5cd9-904b-c28bf7b1983d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082530} {:answer/id #uuid "2b8eea8e-ad14-5188-8047-403f58171561" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5}{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082531}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "2b83c98e-2aad-5cd9-904b-c28bf7b1983d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082530} :answer-field/id #uuid "dce201e3-8954-5096-9c90-ef449aabac90" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082526}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "234add05-86f7-55a3-868f-ff02274a16b2" :question/math-academy-id "q-85665" :question/problem "Compute the arc length of $y=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})$ for $x\\in [0,\\operatorname{ln}⁡2].$" :question/worked-solution "The arc length of a curve $y=f(x)$ between $x=a$ and $x=b$ can be found using the formula\n\n$$\nL={\\int }_{a}^{b}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, $f(x)=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x}),$ $a=0,$ and $b=\\operatorname{ln}⁡2.$\n\nFirst, we find the derivative of $f(x):$\n\n$$\n{f}^{′}(x)=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})\n$$\n\nNow, let's compute $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}} & =\\sqrt{1+{[\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x})]}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{1+\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2x}-2+{e}^{-2x})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}({e}^{2x}+2+{e}^{-2x})} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{\\frac{1}{4}{({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting $\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})$ to the formula for the arc length, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}\\sqrt{1+[{f}^{′}(x)]^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{\\int }_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}({e}^{x}+{e}^{-x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{x}-{e}^{-x}){|}_{0}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[({e}^{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2}-{e}^{-\\operatorname{ln}⁡2})-({e}^{0}-{e}^{-0})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}[2-\\frac{1}{2}-0] \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
